WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.560
Hold your breath. Carbon dioxide accumulates, and oxygen falls.

2
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:13.620
Yet what defeats you is mostly the carbon dioxide piling up, not oxygen running out.

3
00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.840
Why is the controller built that way?

4
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:21.593
Two questions: where does the rhythm come from, and what sets how hard it drives the

5
00:00:21.593 --> 00:00:26.453
muscles? The rhythm comes from the medulla, from a small region called the pre

6
00:00:26.453 --> 00:00:27.533
-Botzinger complex.

7
00:00:28.453 --> 00:00:32.153
Smith and colleagues found it in a neonatal rat brainstem in a dish.

8
00:00:32.673 --> 00:00:36.153
That is a reduced preparation, not the adult human circuit.

9
00:00:36.653 --> 00:00:38.733
At rest, the drive is largely chemical.

10
00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:44.927
Carbon dioxide crosses the blood-brain barrier and releases hydrogen ions in brain

11
00:00:44.927 --> 00:00:49.227
fluid. The brain mainly senses those ions, not the molecule itself.

12
00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:54.367
In mice, retrotrapezoid neurons are proton-sensitive and project to the pre

13
00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:55.367
-Botzinger complex.

14
00:00:55.947 --> 00:00:58.147
Which cells do the sensing is still open.

15
00:00:58.647 --> 00:01:01.827
The carotid body sits at the fork of each carotid artery.

16
00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:07.767
It is the main peripheral chemoreceptor for arterial oxygen, carbon dioxide and pH.

17
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:14.090
Against arterial PCO2, human ventilation rises along a roughly straight line.

18
00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:20.070
Against alveolar PO2, the curve is shallow above about 60 millimetres of mercury.

19
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.190
It steepens as it enters roughly 50 to 60.

20
00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.690
Weil and colleagues measured it under isocapnia.

21
00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:32.810
Added inspired carbon dioxide held alveolar PCO2 at its resting value.

22
00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:38.050
Without that clamp, breathing harder lowers PCO2, which restrains ventilation.

23
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.380
Take an adult at rest at sea level.

24
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.320
Arterial PCO2 is about 40 millimetres of mercury.

25
00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.180
Arterial PO2 is about 100.

26
00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:53.120
That is the steep part of the carbon dioxide line, and the flat part of the oxygen

27
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.720
curve. A small PCO2 error gives a large ventilation change.

28
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.700
A small PO2 error gives almost none.

29
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.840
Oxygen's leverage lies elsewhere on its axis.

30
00:02:04.683 --> 00:02:07.063
The breath hold ends at the apnea break point.

31
00:02:07.723 --> 00:02:12.423
The carbon dioxide signal usually arrives first, though no simple blood gas rule

32
00:02:12.423 --> 00:02:13.703
fully explains it.

33
00:02:14.163 --> 00:02:18.963
Eighteen volunteers with little freediving experience held repeated maximal breaths,

34
00:02:18.963 --> 00:02:20.603
face in cold water.

35
00:02:21.343 --> 00:02:26.563
Fifteen seconds of hyperventilation lowered starting end tidal PCO2.

36
00:02:27.143 --> 00:02:33.143
It fell from 29.0 to 17.4 millimetres of mercury in the thirteen with usable gas

37
00:02:33.143 --> 00:02:38.143
data. The mean hold grew from 111 to 133 seconds.

38
00:02:38.823 --> 00:02:44.083
The mean saturation nadir fell from 93.6 to 90.6 percent.

39
00:02:44.703 --> 00:02:49.683
Hyperventilation lowers the carbon dioxide signal and adds almost no oxygen.

40
00:02:50.283 --> 00:02:54.003
In an adult at rest, breathing is a carbon dioxide controller.
